3. THE REAL MODE BASIS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3.1 Self-conjugate and conjugate momenta

Lemma 3.1 (Momentum pair structure). The 14 momenta at lambda = 4 split into
two self-conjugate momenta {(0,4), (4,0)} (each satisfying
(n_1, n_2) = (-n_1, -n_2) mod 8) and six conjugate pairs
{(1,3),(7,5)}, {(1,5),(7,3)}, {(3,1),(5,7)}, {(3,7),(5,1)}, {(2,2),(6,6)},
{(2,6),(6,2)}.

Proof. Direct computation. A momentum (n_1,n_2) is self-conjugate iff
2n_1 = 0 and 2n_2 = 0 (mod 8), i.e., n_1, n_2 in {0,4}. Among the 14 momenta,
only (0,4) and (4,0) have both coordinates in {0,4}. The remaining 12 momenta
form six conjugate pairs. []

3.2 Cosine and sine modes

Definition 3.2 (Real modes). For momentum (n_1, n_2) in Z_8 x Z_8, define the
cosine mode and the sine mode

    C_{n_1,n_2}(x_1, x_2) = cos(pi (n_1 x_1 + n_2 x_2) / 4) / ||c_{n_1,n_2}||,
    S_{n_1,n_2}(x_1, x_2) = sin(pi (n_1 x_1 + n_2 x_2) / 4) / ||s_{n_1,n_2}||,

where ||c||^2 = sum_x c(x)^2 and ||s||^2 = sum_x s(x)^2 are the Euclidean
normalizations on the 64 sites.

3.3 Real mode basis theorem

Theorem 3.3 (Real mode basis). The lambda = 4 eigenspace of the 5-point
Laplacian on Lambda = Z_8 x Z_8 is 14-dimensional and admits the orthonormal
basis

    {C_{0,4}, C_{4,0}} union (union over the six conjugate pairs of
    Lemma 3.1) {C_n, S_n}.

Proof. Each C_n and S_n satisfies Delta C_n = lambda(n) C_n and
Delta S_n = lambda(n) S_n because the Laplacian is diagonalized by the complex
plane waves and C_n, S_n are its real and imaginary parts (up to
normalization). For n in the 14-momentum list, lambda(n) = 4, so each listed
function is an eigenvector at lambda = 4. Linear independence follows from
orthogonality of the complex plane waves at distinct momenta and orthogonality
of cos and sin at the same momentum. The count is 2 + 6*2 = 14. Since the
multiplicity is 14 (HCSM-04, Theorem 3.2), these functions span the
eigenspace. []

