5. ORBIT DECOMPOSITION AND STABILIZERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5.1 Orbits

Theorem 5.1 (D_4 orbit decomposition). The 14 modes at lambda = 4 decompose
under D_4 into three orbits

    O_0 = {(0,4), (4,0)}, |O_0| = 2,
    O_1 = {(1,3), (1,5), (3,1), (3,7), (5,1), (5,7), (7,3), (7,5)}, |O_1| = 8,
    O_2 = {(2,2), (2,6), (6,2), (6,6)}, |O_2| = 4.

Proof. Direct computation. Application of the generators r and s to a
representative of each orbit reproduces the listed sets. []

5.2 Stabilizers

Theorem 5.2 (Orbit stabilizers). The stabilizers of the three orbits,
evaluated at the representatives (0,4), (1,3), (2,2), have orders

    |Stab(O_0)| = 4, |Stab(O_1)| = 1, |Stab(O_2)| = 2.

They satisfy the orbit-stabilizer identity
|O_k| = |D_4| / |Stab(O_k)| = 8 / |Stab(O_k)|.

Proof. Stabilizer of O_0 at (0,4). The elements r^2, s, and r^2 s all fix
(0,4): r^2(0,4) = (0,4) (since 4 = -4 mod 8), s(0,4) = (0,4),
r^2 s(0,4) = (0,4). No other element fixes (0,4). So
Stab(O_0) = {e, r^2, s, r^2 s}, order 4.

Stabilizer of O_1 at (1,3). Direct check: r(1,3) = (5,1) != (1,3),
s(1,3) = (1,5) != (1,3), and the remaining five non-identity elements also
map (1,3) to distinct momenta. So Stab(O_1) = {e}, order 1.

Stabilizer of O_2 at (2,2). r^2(2,2) = (6,6) != (2,2), s(2,2) = (2,6) != (2,2),
but rs(2,2) = (2,2) exactly. So Stab(O_2) = {e, rs}, order 2. []

