7. THE n_1-PARITY OPERATOR
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

7.1 The n_1-parity operator

Definition 7.1 (n_1-parity operator). On the 14-mode multiplet, in the
standard mode order, define the diagonal operator

    D = diag((-1)^(n_1^(i))), i = 1,...,14.

Its entries are +1 if the mode's first momentum component n_1 is even and -1
if n_1 is odd.

7.2 The parity constraint at lambda = 4

Lemma 7.2 (n_1 = n_2 mod 2 at lambda = 4). Every mode (n_1, n_2) satisfying
cos(pi n_1 / 4) + cos(pi n_2 / 4) = 0 satisfies n_1 = n_2 (mod 2).

Proof. The values of cos(pi n / 4) for n in {0,...,7} are
1, sqrt(2)/2, 0, -sqrt(2)/2, -1, -sqrt(2)/2, 0, sqrt(2)/2.
For even n, cos(pi n / 4) in {+1, 0, -1}. For odd n, cos(pi n / 4) in
{+sqrt(2)/2, -sqrt(2)/2}. The sum cos(pi n_1 / 4) + cos(pi n_2 / 4) vanishes
iff the two cosines are of the same type and opposite signs: either both in
{+1, 0, -1} (giving 1 - 1 = 0 or 0 - 0 = 0) or both in {+sqrt(2)/2,
-sqrt(2)/2} (giving sqrt(2)/2 - sqrt(2)/2 = 0). Hence n_1 and n_2 have the
same parity. []

7.3 The n_1-parity orbit indicator

Theorem 7.3 (D is the orbit indicator). On the 14-mode multiplet,

    D = +1 on O_0 + O_2 = {nu, e, t, W, Z, H},
    D = -1 on O_1 = {u, d, s, mu, dark, c, tau, b}.

Equivalently, D = 2 P_{O_0 + O_2} - I = I - 2 P_{O_1}.

Proof. By Lemma 7.2, every mode at lambda = 4 satisfies n_1 = n_2 (mod 2).
The orbits are defined by the parity of (n_1, n_2): O_0 + O_2 consists of the
6 modes with n_1, n_2 both even; O_1 consists of the 8 modes with n_1, n_2
both odd. Therefore (-1)^(n_1) = +1 on O_0 + O_2 and -1 on O_1. []

Corollary 7.4 (D is D_4-invariant). [D, R_g] = 0 for all g in D_4, since D is
a linear combination of the orbit projectors P_{O_0 + O_2} and P_{O_1}, both
of which are D_4-invariant.

7.4 Commutation with the orientation flip

The orientation flip is F: (n_1, n_2) -> (n_2, n_1) (HCSM-43). It acts on the
multiplet by F(O_k) = O_k setwise.

Theorem 7.5 ([D,F] = 0). The operators D and F commute on the 14-mode
multiplet.

Proof. Since D = 2 P_{O_0 + O_2} - I (Theorem 7.3) and
F(O_0 + O_2) = O_0 + O_2 (HCSM-43, Theorem 3.1), F commutes with
P_{O_0 + O_2}, hence with D. []

