8. THE PROCESSOR AND THE CLASS PARTITION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This section derives the processor T and the class partition C from the
winding generator H_wind and the fermion/boson homogeneity constraint. The
previously disclosed input (P8) is thereby closed.

8.1 The winding generator

Definition 8.1 (Winding matrix and winding generator). Let n_1, n_2 in
R^14 be the coordinate vectors of the 14 modes. The winding matrix is the
rank-2 antisymmetric bilinear

    M_{ij} = n_1^(i) n_2^(j) - n_1^(j) n_2^(i).

The winding generator is

    H_wind = i sin(pi M / 8),

with sin applied elementwise.

Theorem 8.2 (Winding generator properties; HCSM-14). The winding generator
H_wind is Hermitian, with entries in Q(sqrt(2)). Its characteristic polynomial
is

    p(lambda) = det(lambda I_14 - H_wind) = lambda^10 (lambda^2 - 8)(lambda^2 - 32).

Its spectrum is {-4 sqrt(2), -2 sqrt(2), 0^(10), +2 sqrt(2), +4 sqrt(2)}. Its
kernel has dimension 10.

Proof. See HCSM-14, Theorem 3.8 and Corollary 4.4. The result is derived from
the coordinate vectors of the 14 modes and the antisymmetry of M. []

8.2 The winding class partition

Theorem 8.3 (Winding class partition; HCSM-14, HCSM-28). The 14 modes at
lambda = 4 partition into three winding classes

    A = {nu, e, t}, B = {u, d, s, mu, c, tau, b}, C = {dark, W, Z, H},

of sizes (3, 7, 4), uniquely determined by the D_4 orbit partition together
with the single two-mode swap {t, dark}.

Proof. By HCSM-28, Theorem 3.1 (Orbit-Class Reconstruction Theorem), the
winding class partition is the D_4 orbit partition modified by the unique
two-mode swap that makes each class fermion/boson homogeneous. The D_4 orbit
partition is

    O_0 = {nu, e}, O_1 = {u, d, s, mu, dark, c, tau, b}, O_2 = {W, Z, H, t}.

The fermion/boson split is

    F = {nu, e, u, d, s, mu, c, tau, b, t}, |F| = 10,
    B_bos = {dark, W, Z, H}, |B_bos| = 4.

A (3,7,4) fermion/boson-homogeneous partition must have bosonic class
C = B_bos = {dark, W, Z, H}. To agree with the orbit partition on 12 of 14
modes, exactly one fermion must switch classes: t (the only fermion in O_2)
moves to class A, and dark (the only boson in O_1) moves to class C. This
swap is unique. []

8.3 The processor

Definition 8.4 (Processor). The processor is the rank-10 orthogonal projection

    T = I - P_C = P_A + P_B.

Theorem 8.5 (Fermion/boson-class correspondence; HCSM-13). The processor T
commutes with the class-C projector P_C and equals T = I - P_C = P_A + P_B.
Equivalently, im(T) = A + B = F and ker(T) = C = B_bos.

Proof. Direct from Definition 8.4 and Theorem 8.3. The processor is diagonal
in the standard mode basis with

    diag(T) = (1,1,1,1,1,1,0,1,1,1,0,0,0,1).

The image is the fermion sector F = A + B; the kernel is the boson sector
C = B_bos. []

Corollary 8.6 (Derived processor). The processor T and the class partition C
are derived from the winding generator H_wind and the fermion/boson
homogeneity constraint. The derivation chain is

    lattice primitive -> H_wind -> {A,B,C} -> T = I - P_C.

The disclosed input (P8) of the previous revision is closed.

8.4 Commutation with the processor

Theorem 8.7 ([D,T] = 0). The operators D and T commute on the 14-mode
multiplet.

Proof. D is diagonal in the standard mode basis (Definition 7.1), and T is
diagonal in the standard mode basis (Theorem 8.5). Hence they commute. []

