9. THE JOINT (D,T) SPECTRUM
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Theorem 9.1 ((D,T) class partition). The joint eigenspaces of (D,T) on the
14-mode multiplet are

    (D = +1, T = 1) = {nu, e, t}, dim 3,
    (D = -1, T = 1) = {u, d, s, mu, c, tau, b}, dim 7,
    (D = +1, T = 0) = {W, Z, H}, dim 3,
    (D = -1, T = 0) = {dark}, dim 1.

Writing class A for the first, class B for the second, and C = (T = 0) for
the union of the third and fourth rows, the 14 modes partition into

    A = {nu, e, t}, B = {u, d, s, mu, c, tau, b}, C = {dark, W, Z, H},

with sizes 3, 7, 4.

Proof. Direct from Theorems 7.3 and 8.5. []

Remark 9.2 (Corrected statement for HCSM-14). The published HCSM-14 states
that the class partition is derived from the winding generator H_wind, "by
frequency". Theorem 9.1 corrects this. The class partition is the joint
spectrum of the n_1-parity D and the processor T. The processor T is itself
derived from H_wind and fermion/boson homogeneity (Theorem 8.5).

Proposition 9.3 (H_wind does not respect the class partition). The commutator
of H_wind with T is nonzero: ||[H_wind, T]||_F = 11.31 (machine-verified).
Consequently, the class partition is not derivable from H_wind alone; it
requires the fermion/boson homogeneity constraint.

