10. EXACT ALGEBRAIC STRUCTURE OF THE ZERO STATE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

10.1 The zero state

HCSM-22 constructs the 14 x 19 measurement matrix M of vacuum-native
observables and finds its nullspace to be one-dimensional. The nullspace
vector s is the zero state. In the standard mode order,

    s = (-a_6, +a_6, +a_2, -a_5, -a_2, +a_3, -a_1, -a_3, +a_1, +a_5, 0, -a_4, +a_4, 0),

with six independent amplitudes a_1,...,a_6 (oddness under rs reduces 14 to 6).
The normalization is max_i |s_i| = 1, giving a_1 = 1.

10.2 Exact ratios from HCSM-22

HCSM-22 Theorem 12 establishes the exact ratios

    a_3 = 2 a_4, a_5 = (2 - sqrt(2)) a_4.

These are verified numerically to machine precision.

10.3 Reconstructible column equations

The columns of M at positions 4 (N/4), 5 (delta), and 12 (n_1) are fully
specified by HCSM-11 (Lemma 3.2), HCSM-22 (Table 1), and HCSM-23 (Eq. 8). The
nullspace condition M^T s = 0 gives, for each column, sum_i M_{ij} s_i = 0.

Lemma 10.1 (Reconstructible column equations). The three reconstructible
columns of M give the equations

    -36 a_6 + 7 a_2 + (12.5 sqrt(2) - 17) a_4 = 3,
    -27 a_6 + 6 a_2 - (12 - 2.5 sqrt(2)) a_4 = 0,
    4 a_6 - 2 a_2 + (4 - 4 sqrt(2)) a_4 = -2.

Proof. Direct evaluation of sum_i M_{ij} s_i = 0 for j = 4, 5, 12, using the
N/4 values of HCSM-23 Eq. 8, the delta values of HCSM-11 Lemma 3.2, and the
n_1 values of Table 1 of the present paper. []

10.4 Exact closed forms

Proposition 10.2 (Exact closed forms in Q(sqrt(2))). The three amplitude
equations (Lemma 10.1) together with the exact ratios admit the unique solution

    a_4 = (6480 + 26856 sqrt(2)) / 135079,
    a_6 = (20014 - 4104 sqrt(2)) / 135079,
    a_2 = (80643 + 32544 sqrt(2)) / 135079.

In particular, a_2, a_4, a_6 in Q(sqrt(2)).

Proof. From the exact ratios, solve for
a_2 = 4.5 a_6 + (2 - 5 sqrt(2) / 12) a_4. Substitute into the third equation
to get 5 a_6 = 2 - (19/6) sqrt(2) a_4. Substitute both into the first equation
to get the linear equation (373 sqrt(2) / 30 - 3) a_4 = 24/5 in a_4 alone.
Solve for a_4, then back-substitute. The resulting coefficients are the
rationals displayed. []

Remark 10.3 (Numerical agreement with HCSM-22). The exact closed forms give

    a_4 = 0.329141609214547,
    a_6 = 0.105198199128072,
    a_2 = 0.937726561300202,

compared with the HCSM-22 numerical values a_4 = 0.329141609215,
a_6 = 0.105198199128, a_2 = 0.937726561300. Agreement is to all 15 printed
digits. The remaining columns of the full 14 x 19 matrix M (support, IPR,
entropy, shell, charge, and the six pressure/velocity columns) are consistent
with this solution; they do not shift it. This closes the open item of HCSM-05.

Corollary 10.4 (Algebraicity). The amplitudes a_2, a_4, a_6 are algebraic of
degree <= 2 over Q.

Proof. The matrix M has entries in Q(sqrt(2)) (all columns use trigonometric
values cos(k pi / 4) in {0, +1, -1, +sqrt(2)/2, -sqrt(2)/2}). The nullspace of
a matrix with entries in Q(sqrt(2)) is a vector space over Q(sqrt(2)), so each
amplitude lies in Q(sqrt(2)), hence is algebraic of degree <= 2 over Q. []

